kids today have all kinds of things to  occupy their minds from tablets to video  games to remote control toys but one  company aims to give them something to  awaken their imaginations  after a wildly successful Kickstarter  campaign candy lab toys now makes one  simple thing wooden cars they're fun and  hip modern and retro appealing to both  kids and adults I met up with the  talented toy makers in their Brooklyn  New York work space what does it mean to  be a toy maker in 2017 so we're  designers or engineers we are retailers  we have these ideas almost like little  movies that we play in our heads that  represent the seed inspiration of our  stories for anyone used to the toys of  today often prefab and plastic these  little cars feel like there's something  new yet old at the same time they're  wooden handcrafted made with an  exceptional attention to detail and  designed not to occupy the mind but to  inspire it flad where did the idea for  this incredible to a company come from  my wife and I at the time felt that the  toys that we had access to were not  quite engaging in a sense that adults  could actually partake in play we felt  that there's something too guided in  terms of Licensing and a pre-existing  storyline so we figured that there's a  way to combine real cultural cues so the  idea of playing with cars as a kid was  that something you would do absolutely  Vlad Ragusan founded the company his  passion for both design and for cars  were driving forces but developing just  the right look and feel was a long and  winding road the hard part was coming up  with a design because it's always  difficult to see hey what would a kid  like and what would make it compelling  to them to play for a while and what  would be an adult sort of interest to  grown up as a parent to actually partake  in that ko Helder is candy labs  industrial designer he was immediately  drawn to the company for its compelling  designs right off the bat there is the  beautiful colors I think that's a really  I think that's a really key thing that  really helps them pop off the shelf and  then when you dig in deeper there's a  lot of small details that are very well  considered so you have small iconic  moments pulled from actual cars that you  can see and then this body can be  flipped from the very first model each  car has been designed and crafted not to  duplicate an actual car but to magically  capture its essence we designed this  kind of sports car orange car that we we  started out with and that has a stance  where the nose is sort of diving down a  little bit and the rear is sort of  lifted up that's because at that era in  that particular model that I was  inspired by which is in 1967 they have  spring Leafs on the back and they had a  heavy engine in the front which  naturally after a few years the car  starts to dive down a bit you're talking  about such specific details in a car  than a child  play with so it really is important to  you that it's attractive to an adult  as well it is important because  obviously a five-year-old wouldn't know  about a 65 GTO but his father or his  grandfather could tell him because they  will notice that but that's exactly the  point of our toys we want to have that  connection to real-world experiences  this is actually carved out of a solid  block they hope these elements help  create a context that enriches a child's  experience beyond just playtime we want  to give them enough of a backstory that  are interesting but not too much of a  backstory because we want them to still  have their own imagination kind of play  a big role in this when we start  designing we do start putting pen to  paper  the idea for each product line starts  with a sketch then a prototype is made  using 3d printing from there a wooden  model is constructed it can take up to  30 tries before the final version is  complete and the design team is always  looking for inspiration I think  throughout the entire process we're  keeping an eye on the research looking  at photos of existing cars going to  events if we can where we can see these  things in person and finding bits of  inspiration from that cultural era that  were inspired by from the numbers  painted on the side to the fabrics they  choose even the real rubber tires  everything is important to the stories  these toy makers want to tell when a  family buys a car what do you hope they  get out of that moment we hope they get  hours of fun and we hope they get some  really interesting conversations about  what these cars represent as an idea and  we hope that they remember some road  trips and maybe reenact that on their  play mat and we think that is the  essence of play establishing that  connection that moment that allows you  to recreate some moments that you're  cherish  and we've got the new collection here  the outlaws with us or five in this  series they're great and by the way pick  them up these are heavy cars play with  them  prices range $24.99 all the way up to  above a hundred for the collectors items  and I'm telling you every adult that's  picked these up has done that you got to  play with them and I love the fact they  recognize adults like toys - that's  right I like toys on TV maybe we lucky  you can keep one of these things
 

